    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Hello Black Like Me podcast family. I hope that you're doing well. I just wanted to do a brief introduction to this very exciting episode. I have the honor of having the honorable Tammy Baldwin on Black Like Me. Tammy Baldwin hails from the state of Wisconsin. She is a member of the US Senate and she has served our community in so many different ways. To a credit, I appreciate the fact that she, as a very prominent member and the Democratic Party, reached out to me because of comments I said on Zoom calls and in speeches that said I really want Democrats to not take black voters for granted and to make sure that in addition to doing what's got to happen in November that they're thinking about the Black agenda. Her office reached out to me. She agreed to be on my podcast without a moment of hesitation. So I just want to say a couple of things here. I want you to listen really close to this conversation because I asked some very pointed questions. So I want you to think about what did you hear? What did you not hear? I also want you to know that these fine folks work for us. We elect them and they work for us. So I know that people who listen to this podcast vote differently, but you're from every state in the union. Please know that you have access to your elected officials. You can call, you can email, you can write and say, here are my thoughts, here are my concerns. There are plethora of things that they deal with. But if we begin to reach out to those representatives, come on, they're only two per state and say we are concerned about issues that involve black people and issues of my incarceration or issues of economic development, wealth development in the black community. They'll have to listen. So what I want you to understand is Senator Baldwin was very accessible to me and has agreed to even come on again. But what I think she did, what I hope she does, but what I want you to do is to know that you can reach out to the people who you have elected. and ask them these same kinds of questions. They might not come on your podcast, but they do have a responsibility to respond to your inquiries. So enjoy this episode, a very frank and very candid discussion with the honorable Senator Tammy Baldwin. But I want you to listen to this and to understand that we, the people, have a right to direct and inform those whom we have elected. They are representatives. They represent us. So let's not sit silent and complain around the television. Let's help those we've elected to remember what we've elected them for. Enjoy this episode. I enjoyed the discussion. Hey, hey, hey, welcome back to another exciting episode of Black Like Me with Dr. Alex G. Each week it's my duty, it's my responsibility to bring you thought provoking discussions around what's impacting African Americans and African American life in America. And today is no exception. I'm really excited about today's interview for a number of reasons. The honorable Tammy Baldwin, Senator Tammy Baldwin who hails from the state of Wisconsin is my guest today. We're going to be talking about a plethora of things. I've been talking both to black and white Democrats in my city, around the state, who have an interest in what happens to and for black people in America. And so I'm so excited about that. The senator was born and raised in Madison by her grandparents. She was valedictorian from Madison West High School. We'll talk about that in a minute. That's also",
