    "turn_text": "mater, 1980. I'm class of 1981 who were actually in high school together for about three years. She did a double major at Smith College in Mathematics and Political Science. And her first political office was at the age of 24 was a Dane County supervisor in 1986. At that time, that must have been one of the youngest county supervisors to come on board. She'd been a member of the Democratic Party who served three terms in Wisconsin State, assembly representing the 78th district. And from 1999 to 2013, she served in Wisconsin's second congressional district. in the United States House of Representatives. She's been serving us for a good while. In 1988, she became the first openly gay woman and first openly LGBT woman elected to the United States Congress. And in 2012, she was elected to the United States Senate. Her current committees, if they haven't changed since my research committee on appropriations, on commerce, science, and transportation, and also Committee on Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions. And she's also a member of the Congressional Progressive Caucus. I can go on and say so many other things about what she's doing. And I'm sure that will come up in the course of our conversation. But Senator Baldwin, I'm so happy to have you here today. Thank you for being my special guest on Black Like Me.",
