    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "And causes educational inequities, causes health inequities, and then it gets into some policy proposals. So, you know, when we're talking about how do we make it better, it's partly by really digging deep to understand the complexity of the challenges. And I just think because of the extent of segregation, particularly in Milwaukee, but certainly in Madison also, that too often there's just perceptions of other that aren't based on reality. And you got to really get to know people and dig deep to understand that these things are interconnected. I will say, and this may be going a little off the question, but You know, the past months, this year, have really, I think, lifted, let's say, a veil on racism, discrimination, bias, and inequities, disparities, starting with the coronavirus pandemic. And If it weren't for the fact that Milwaukee last year had in their county board declared racism as a public health issue, a public health crisis, Milwaukee was one of the first cities to report out coronavirus cases and no race and ethnicity. And I remember talking to the public health director in Milwaukee who had been interviewed, I think, in the Atlantic magazine of sort of why are you doing this? But for doing this, it might have taken us a lot longer in America to figure out. that this horrendous pandemic was disproportionately impacting African Americans and Latinx and other racial minorities. And so Milwaukee played a very key role Wisconsin in identifying this. And as I said, lifting that veil A lot of people knew that was already going on in terms of",
