  {
    "episode_id": 86127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "Sure. So in our community, Senator, we've known that those things have been real sense. Urban League of Greater Madison had a study out in 2010 called the State of Black Madison, the Race to Equity Report came out in 2013. I wrote an op-ed piece called Justified Anger in 2013. So we've been trying to cry out. So we feel like as a Black community, we've been doing our part. What's the party's response to these unveiled truths now? What's your vision or your hope for the state of Wisconsin that will happen in terms of policy, economics, opportunity, education? How do we make sure that a state whose model is forward, that we don't look at all of these, that we don't look under the cover and move back? Because that's the fear of black people today, that we will look at it. And for many of those things, we're around zero. So how will that translate to policies, ideals, changes for democratic leaders in the state of Wisconsin, concerning the welfare of Black people.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
