    "episode_id": 86127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 64,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I want to take one second to just note about the race to equity in 2013. You know, I was sworn into the United States Senate for the first time in 2013. And one of the things that was really important to me as I put together a staff both in Wisconsin and in Washington DC was to make sure that we were all as a team knowledgeable about what was unveiled in the race to equity. And I remember having an all staff meeting where we had about an hour and a half long presentation on the methodology, all the geeky stuff, but also all the measurements that were taken there and a discussion about what can we do at the national level to help and how do we partner at the state and local level to see improvement. And those sort of discussions are really, really important. Although you just worry that something like that at some point gets put on a shelf and gathers dust rather than becoming a living document and a living set of recommendations that we",
