  {
    "episode_id": 86127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 84,
    "turn_text": "And one thing that I'll just suggest, Senator, is, and whether there'd be some assertion of authority, whether that's from your office or our Poke hands office or someone's office, we've got to assert some authority so that when these happen, when Milwaukee reports on the COVID-19 incident or When there's a new incident in Madison or the homecoming fiasco, there's a video made in there, no black representation. I don't feel that our elected officials from the Democratic Party communicate with us. Again, I'm not a Democrat. I don't assume because If someone hasn't communicated with me, they haven't communicated with someone else. I'm not saying communicating with a black person, but I'm a part, again, of each major political, religious, social, black network. And because I have 30 plus years in this community as a leader, I'm a pastoral favorite to a lot of these folks. So we have lots and lots of discussion. So one of the things I would suggest is town hall meeting, a gathering that when this is revealed, one, how do I elected officials check in with us to ask, how do we keep you safe? You will. How are you weathering this storm? Because my standard word is beleaguered, whether it's a good day or not. because I can have a great day being dialed into a conversation with the governor about what's going on. My organization, Mia Maya, through our initiative, Justified Angry does training on cross-cultural leadership. We were just contracted by a municipality. I won't say it publicly because they might not have announced it yet, but an entire city in Wisconsin has contracted us to work with them to get law enforcement, community interactions. So we're very involved. But I don't feel like anyone has stopped and said, business leaders, are you watching this? What are you doing? What's the responsibility for the big heavy hitters, the big manufacturers, the institutions? I don't feel like anyone calls them to the carpet to say, what's your role in making this better? When there's a shooting senator, when there's looting, when there's a perception that black people are burning downplace like State Street, I am called like fat. rev, we need you, the governor needs you, the kind of executive needs you, most recently, the mayor needs you, but I'm never called on about water issues. I'm never, my doctorate is in leadership. I'm never called on about leadership issues, but if the black people are getting out of hand in the community, then I'm going to the big house. And, but we're not checked in on, we're sitting here and we're wondering things like, does I'm listening, I want that to go to another question about some things I'm hearing about from black voters, but what can you or your colleagues in the Democratic Party do to bring big business and community leaders who happen to be black and people of color to discuss the economic impact of COVID-19? impact of all of this, the emotional impact of watching George Floyd murdered on television. How does it affect our ability to communicate hope to those who are coming behind us? We're losing black people from the state of Wisconsin. I've got friends who work in state government for their whole careers and they are building homes as we speak in North Carolina, in Mississippi. They're moving to Texas because they don't feel that Wisconsin is forward for them. So in light of all the covers being back, pulled back. I know there can be laws and rules, but Senator in all honesty, they're not even conversations about how this is impacting us. And our mayor has come out with a statement that racial stress and microaggressions have become a social determinants of health. Where actually my organization is under contract with the School of Medicine and Public Policy to decrease those microaggressions by training white allies to understand how to work and create more equitable environments for black people in business, state government, campus, even in their own communities. So we know that this is real too, but no one. has pulled us together and pulled business leaders and governmental leaders because we don't change policies. We don't have the money or the resource or even the political goodwill or pool to do it. And the two entities who could business and government are never in communication with us about what we see, what we need and what we feel. And I feel that Senator is killing black people who are already at risk. And who's sounding that alarm?",
