    "turn_text": "Yeah, but I think that I say a couple of things. One, and I certainly were talking in the context still of a public health pandemic. When we have been working on legislation at the federal level, we have in terms of pandemic response, I know that there's certainly some things we've gotten right and there's some things we've not gotten right. I think that one of the issues that I've heard a lot about that sort of involves government, business and communities is the small business program that was stood up. And everyone was very hopeful about being able to give a loan or a grant to entrepreneurs and small businesses to help them get through. And when that program first opened up, lo and behold, all of the folks who had accounts at the big banks and loans from the big banks were first in line. And it didn't take very long to go through a lot of money and find out that our businesses owned by Black entrepreneurs or in neighborhoods that are Black neighborhoods predominantly or Latinx community, we're not getting the funding. And I remember meeting with the Wisconsin African American Chamber of Commerce, a Zoom call, you know,",
