    "turn_idx": 102,
    "turn_text": "I can't, but you or your office could say, there's going to be a discussion and we're going to have some representation because we have an opportunity to change things in Wisconsin. And I think it's important that we do. There are a couple of questions I had is I just talked with people in my community who are African-American and so who are African-American. And I just had a couple of them. One gentleman said, he says, I'm a black professional. He said, but my peers and I largely feel that our democratic party is ignoring us. because they assume we're just going to vote. Someone else said, someone said, wealthy middle class and black folks are ready for a plan or an agenda that strengthens the black community. They want something in someone they can follow. Another person says, they just believe that our party is disinterested. I'll just pause right there and just say, what would you like to say to black voters who are going to be listening to this? who want to see change, they want to vote for their parents' party. And these are thinking, voting, folks. Often when we talk about the Black community, we often talk about distress and disenfranchisement, and that's real. My organization addresses that. I'm a product of that reality. My mom moved us here to change that. But I reached out particularly to voting African Americans who are middle class to wealthy. And they feel that our votes are already assumed. And in a state that is considered a swing state, Senator, we can't afford for black people to be disinterested. We can't afford black people to say, well, I'm not going to the polls and not open. And I'm not going to mail them this absentee vote. So I want to give you a moment, Senator. What do you want to say to black people? who feel we're being overlooked. Let me ask that in two ways Senator, sorry about that. My head has been in so quickly. My first question is I wouldn't expect you to say yes, we've ignored black folks. So I'm not trying to put you in that position. But I guess what I'm asking is can you respect or understand the perception that black people feel ignored in this state by what I call just from right now a party that probably most black voting folks have supported. And secondly, I want to give you a chance to speak directly to your black",
